    "description": "Gilaki is a Northwestern Iranian language of the Caspian subgroup, associated with Gilan in northern Iran. Its eastern and western varieties and distinctive grammatical structures show why it should be understood as a language with internal diversity rather than simply a regional Persian accent. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Gilaki", "Gilan", "Caspian languages", "Iranian languages", "Grammar"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T09:32:13Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T09:32:13Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Gilaki: Gilan's Caspian Language | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Gilaki's eastern and western varieties, its place among Caspian languages, and the grammar that distinguishes it from Persian."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "gilaki-in-gilan",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Gilaki within a multilingual province",
        "content": "Gilaki belongs to the Caspian subgroup of Northwestern Iranian. Its association with Gilan, on Iran's southern Caspian coast, should not turn the province into an imagined single-language territory. Donald Stilo's survey also identifies Talyshi, Rudbari, Tati, Kurdish, and non-Iranian languages in Gilan. A provincial name is therefore a geographic starting point, rather than a complete description of the language used by every resident. [s1]\n\nStilo distinguishes eastern and western Gilaki, with the Sefidrud River serving as their general dividing line. Toward Mazandaran, the picture becomes more complicated: transitional varieties around Tonekabon and Kelardasht do not fit neatly into a simple Gilaki–Mazandarani division. His account argues that their limited mutual intelligibility with either language merits treating them separately. This is a scholarly classification proposal, rather than a reason to erase speakers' own local labels. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "grammatical-structure",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A different way to build a phrase",
        "content": "Grammar provides a concrete route beyond the idea of Gilaki as accented Persian. Mansour Shabani and Sahar Taghipour's study examines the Eshkevarat variety, rather than claiming to describe every Gilaki dialect. Its published abstract reports that noun phrases and most adpositional phrases are head-final: the central word follows material that depends on it. [s2]\n\nThe study also describes a linking element called “Reverse Ezafe.” An ezafe is a grammatical connector within a phrase. In the construction discussed here, certain modifiers or the complement of a postposition are linked to a following head. A postposition performs a role comparable to an English preposition but follows its complement. The authors distinguish the properties of this linker in possessive and adpositional constructions from its behavior with attributive adjectives. These findings show that even a small connecting element can reveal substantial internal organization. [s2]\n\nThe important qualification is local scope. A description of Eshkevarat supplies evidence about that variety; extending every detail to Rasht, Lahijan, or another community would require additional evidence. Readers comparing learning materials should therefore look for the locality represented as well as the name Gilaki. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "contact-and-continuity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Contact without linguistic sameness",
        "content": "Stilo documents extensive Persian influence on Gilaki while also identifying grammatical contrasts and retained vocabulary. Borrowing and structural change do not, by themselves, make the languages identical. His survey also records Gilaki literary and newspaper activity, although its publication history means it cannot establish the extent of such activity today. [s1]\n\nThe most useful introduction to Gilaki therefore combines a regional map with a local grammatical description. The map explains variation; the grammar shows how speakers organize meaning. Together, they offer a more precise account than either a provincial stereotype or a claim that all Caspian speech is interchangeable. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
